title: Revisions and operations
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
aliases: revision operation op_type origin label
summary: An operation is one atomic change; a revision groups the operations of one request, with its time, an optional label and who asked for it.

An ''operation'' is one atomic change — setting one field on one metarecord. It is the
node of the history tree and the unit a navigation crosses.

A ''revision'' groups the operations of one request. Most hold one operation (a field
edited); batch commands make large ones: a set over K matches is K operations, a
reconcile creating M files M, a watcher `Remove` of a directory with N descendants N+1
`file_deleted`. The revision is the smallest unit a rollback moves by (a navigation to an
operation id can stop inside one); undoing one operation of a revision in place is a
revert's job ([[Revert]]).

!! Operation types

|!Type |!Change |!On the filesystem |
|`create_metarecord` |a metarecord created |— |
|`delete_metarecord` |a metarecord deleted, its fields with it |— |
|`set_metarecord` |a metarecord's whole field set replaced |— |
|`set_field` |every row of one field name replaced |— |
|`append_field` |one row appended |— |
|`delete_field` |one or several rows of a name removed |— |
|`file_deleted` |watcher: the file is gone, `mfr_path` → `nothing` |the content is gone |
|`file_moved` |watcher: renamed or moved, `mfr_path` changed |a move to undo |
|`file_modified` |watcher: the content changed, hashes dropped |the old content is gone |

Whether an operation can be undone ''on the filesystem'' is decided when it is undone:
the metadata ones always; `file_deleted` and `file_modified` only if the content is in
the trash (the change went through it — an `mf trash -f`, an earlier rollback),
otherwise only the metadata comes back, and the truth is written again right after;
`file_moved` only if the file is still where the move left it ([[Filesystem coordination]]).

!! Origin

A revision records ''who asked for it'' — `origin`:

* `watcher` — written by the daemon on the filesystem's behalf: the watcher's flush, and the restoration replay after a skipped navigation step;
* `trash` — the metarecord deletion a trashing writes ([[POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/trash]]);
* none — every other write a client requested.

The daemon sets it from ''which endpoint was called'', never from a value a client
sends: a client able to name its origin could pass its writes off as the daemon's, out
of undo's reach. It is the revision that carries it because the operation types cannot:
a file ''arriving'' is a `create_metarecord` plus field writes, indistinguishable by type
from a metarecord you created.

Only `watcher` puts a revision out of undo's reach — not "any origin that is set":
`trash` exists so a client deciding whether to bring bytes back can tell a trashing from
an ordinary deletion, while it stays an ordinary, undoable revision
([[Trash, undo and redo]]). The GUI's [[log panel]] dims the watcher's revisions.

!! Label

A revision may carry a ''label'', a human name that serves as a rollback target: the most
recent revision carrying it (labels are not unique). Set it with the write, or later
(`PATCH /log/revisions/:id`). A labelled revision can also stop the retention trim ([[Automatic retention]]).
